  "name": "Question 14",
  "problem": "A data set contains the observations ${x}_{1}=0.5,{x}_{2}=4.5$ with $z$-scores ${z}_{1}=-3$ and ${z}_{2}=5,$ respectively. Find the standard deviation $\\sigma $ of the data set.\n\n$\\sigma =$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "To compute the value of an observation $x$ which yields a given $z$-score in a data set with mean $\\overset{\u2015}{x}$ and standard deviation $\\sigma ,$ we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\nx=\\overset{\u2015}{x}+z\\cdot \\sigma \n$$\n\nFrom the first observation, we have\n\n$$\n0.5=\\overset{\u00af}{x}+(-3)\\cdot \\sigma .\n$$\n\nFrom the second observation, we have\n\n$$\n4.5=\\overset{\u00af}{x}+5\\cdot \\sigma .\n$$\n\nSubtracting the first equation from the second and solving for $\\sigma ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4.5-0.5 & =(5-(-3))\\cdot \\sigma  \\\\ 4 & =8\\cdot \\sigma  \\\\ \\sigma  & =0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
